  OPTIMIZED LINUX FILE SYSTEM INCLUDING SUITABLE PARTIZIONING UTILIZATION
 A server has a total storage capacity of 50 GB. It needs to store system files, user files, application data and rapidly growing log files. If log files grow continuously, they may use all the available disk space and cause important services to stop. So, proper partitioning and log management are required.
PROPOSED PARTIONING LAYOUT:
otal Disk = 50 GB

/boot       =  1 GB    -> Boot and kernel files
/           =  8 GB    -> Linux operating system
/home       = 12 GB    -> User files
/var        =  8 GB    -> Application data
/var/log    = 10 GB    -> System and application logs
swap        =  4 GB    -> Virtual memory
Free Space  =  7 GB    -> Emergency use and future growth

Total       = 50 GB
Keeping /var/log separate is important because even if logs become very large, they cannot consume the space of the root filesystem.

Using LVM
LVM (Logical Volume Manager) can be used because it provides flexible storage management and makes it easier to resize partitions later.
  
DISK MONITORING:
df -h /var/log
du -sh /var/log/*
journalctl --disk-usage
CONCLUSION:
Conclusion
A separate 10 GB /var/log filesystem, LVM, logrotate, compression and regular monitoring ensure that logs do not exceed 10 GB and prevent disk exhaustion from affecting critical services.
