Frequent context switching reduces CPU performance because the CPU spends time switching between processes.
  Since hardware cannot be upgraded, Linux scheduler and kernel-level optimizations can be used.
1. Check CPU and Context Switching
top
vmstat 1
pidstat -p <PID> 1
These commands help identify CPU usage and context-switching activity.

2. Scheduler Priority
Use nice and renice to control process priority.
sudo renice +10 -p <PID>
For real-time applications, use real-time scheduling:
sudo chrt -f 80 ./realtime_app
SCHED_FIFO gives the real-time process high priority. It should be used carefully because it can affect other processes.

  3. CPU Affinity
A process can be assigned to specific CPU cores:
sudo taskset -cp 2,3 <PID>
This reduces unnecessary CPU migration and can improve performance.

4. Kernel Modules
Check currently loaded modules:
lsmod
Get module information:
modinfo <module>
Remove an unnecessary module if it is safe:
sudo modprobe -r <module>
Only required modules should be loaded to reduce unnecessary resource usage.

5. Real-Time Kernel
For applications requiring very low latency, a PREEMPT_RT kernel can be used.
  It improves kernel responsiveness and is suitable for real-time workloads.

Conclusion
Scheduler priority using chrt and renice, CPU affinity using taskset, proper kernel-module management and a real-time kernel can reduce context-switching delays and improve real-time performance without upgrading hardware.
