Processing 1 million entries within 2 minutes requires efficient commands.
We should avoid processing each line using separate commands because it creates high overhead.
awk is suitable because it can process the complete file in a single pass.
Efficient Script
#!/bin/bash
time LC_ALL=C awk '
{
    count[$9]++
}
END {
    for (code in count)
        print code, count[code]
}' access.log > result.txt

The above script counts HTTP status codes from the log file.

Filtering Errors
LC_ALL=C grep -F "ERROR" application.log |
awk '{count[$1]++}
END {
    for (x in count)
        print x, count[x]
}'

Why These Commands?
awk processes large files in a single pass.
grep -F provides fast fixed-string searching.
LC_ALL=C reduces locale-processing overhead.
Avoiding while read loops and repeated external commands improves speed.
time checks whether processing finishes within 2 minutes.
Conclusion:
Using awk and grep -F with single-pass processing provides an efficient shell-based solution for processing 1 million log entries.
  The execution time should be tested using time to ensure it stays below 120 seconds.


